---
/*
 * Secrets (crates/shep-core/src/secrets.rs, crates/shep-cli/src/commands/secret.rs,
 * docs/brainstorming/specs/2026-09-06-secrets-store-design.md). Every
 * transcript below was run for real against target/release/shep, in a
 * throwaway $SHEP_HOME, on 2026-09-06: the set/list/get/unset sequence
 * first, then a real daemon with one app (web) whose Flockfile references
 * {{secret:DB_PASSWORD}} and resolves it, then a second daemon with two
 * apps that do not (cron, referencing {{secret:REPORT_KEY}} in staging;
 * worker, referencing {{secret:vercel/API_KEY}}), to catch both refusal
 * shapes and both `shep describe` verdicts live rather than typing them
 * from the source.
 */
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import ReferencePills from "../../components/docs/ReferencePills.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/docs/Callout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/docs/CodeBlock.astro";

const storeFile = `$ cat $SHEP_HOME/secrets.json
{
  "version": 1,
  "entries": {
    "DB_PASSWORD": {
      "all": "hunter2",
      "staging": "staging-pw"
    }
  }
}`;

const flockfileSnippet = `[[app]]
name   = "web"
script = "./server.sh"

[app.env]
PW = "{{secret:DB_PASSWORD}}"`;

const spawnedEnv = `$ shep bleats web --no-follow
web | PW=hunter2
web | SHEP_ENVIRONMENT=production`;

const cliDemo = `$ echo "$DB_PASSWORD" | shep secret set DB_PASSWORD --stdin
KEY          ENVIRONMENT
DB_PASSWORD  all

$ echo "$STAGING_PASSWORD" | shep secret set DB_PASSWORD --env staging --stdin
KEY          ENVIRONMENT
DB_PASSWORD  staging

$ shep secret list
KEY          ENVIRONMENTS
DB_PASSWORD  all, staging

$ shep secret get DB_PASSWORD
error[invalid_config]: printing a stored secret back is off; add \`[secrets]\` with \`allow_read = true\` to $SHEP_HOME/shep.toml
$ echo $?
4`;

const gateOpenDemo = `$ echo -e '[secrets]\\nallow_read = true' >> $SHEP_HOME/shep.toml
$ shep secret get DB_PASSWORD
hunter2
$ shep secret get DB_PASSWORD --env staging
staging-pw`;

const unsetDemo = `$ shep secret unset DB_PASSWORD --env staging
KEY          ENVIRONMENT
DB_PASSWORD  staging

$ shep secret unset DB_PASSWORD --env staging
error[not_found]: \`DB_PASSWORD\` has no value for \`staging\`
$ echo $?
3`;

const cronSnippet = `[[app]]
name        = "cron"
script      = "./nightly-report.sh"
environment = "staging"

[app.env]
KEY = "{{secret:REPORT_KEY}}"`;

const refusalLog = `2026-09-06T21:59:39.459750Z  WARN shep_daemon::supervisor: no process was started id=1 reason=\`KEY\`: \`{{secret:REPORT_KEY}}\` has no value in the \`staging\` environment

$ shep flock
ID  NAME    STATUS           PID  RESTARTS  EXIT  CFG  CPU  MEM  UPTIME  FOLD  SMIT
1   cron    errored          -    0         -     -    -    -    0s      -     -
2   worker  waiting-restart  -    0         -     -    -    -    0s      -     -`;

const describeDemo = `$ shep describe cron
ID  NAME  STATUS   PID  RESTARTS  EXIT  CFG  CPU  MEM  UPTIME  FOLD  SMIT
1   cron  errored  -    0         -     -    -    -    0s      -     -

Secrets for cron:
  REPORT_KEY (staging): missing

$ shep describe worker
ID  NAME    STATUS           PID  RESTARTS  EXIT  CFG  CPU  MEM  UPTIME  FOLD  SMIT
2   worker  waiting-restart  -    0         -     -    -    -    0s      -     -

Secrets for worker:
  vercel/API_KEY (production): not cached; a provider may still have it`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Secrets"
  description="A value per key per environment, read by a {{secret:NAME}} reference instead of written into the Flockfile itself."
  crumb="Concepts / Secrets"
  activeSlug="secrets"
>
  <article>
    <h1>Secrets</h1>
    <ReferencePills slug="secrets" />
    <p class="lede">
      A Flockfile ships with its secrets blank, because it is committed to
      the app's own repository. The value has to live somewhere, and{" "}
      <code>$SHEP_HOME/secrets.json</code> is that somewhere: one value per
      key per environment, read by a <code>{'{{secret:NAME}}'}</code>{" "}
      reference and never written into the file it fills in.
    </p>

    <h2>Where it lives</h2>
    <p>
      Owner-only, mode <code>0600</code>, the same shape as{" "}
      <a href="/docs/overrides">overrides.json</a> and{" "}
      <a href="/docs/kv">the KV store</a>: a read-modify-rename under an
      exclusive lock, keys in sorted order so two writes of the same content
      land as the same bytes. <code>shep secret set</code> writes it
      directly and never connects to the shepherd, so filling in a value
      before the first <code>shep start</code> is the ordinary order, not a
      workaround.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock>{storeFile}</CodeBlock>

    <h2>The token</h2>
    <p>
      An <code>env</code> value or an arg can carry{" "}
      <code>{'{{secret:NAME}}'}</code>, the same doubled-brace grammar{" "}
      <a href="/docs/first-flockfile">Your first Flockfile</a> covers for{" "}
      <code>{'{{instance}}'}</code> and <code>{'{{name}}'}</code>. Anything
      between the braces that is not a known token is refused at{" "}
      <code>shep start</code>, this one included.
    </p>
    <Callout variant="careful">
      A value rendered into <code>args</code> is visible in{" "}
      <code>ps</code> to every user on the host for as long as the process
      runs, the same exposure <code>shep secret set</code>'s positional
      value carries. shep does not refuse it, because passing configuration
      as an argument is how a great many programs take it; where a program
      reads the same setting from either, <code>env</code> is the one that
      stays between shep and the child.
    </Callout>
    <p class="fine">
      <code>out_file</code> and <code>err_file</code> take the two
      positional tokens and refuse this one. A resolved log path is not
      contained the way a resolved <code>env</code> value is: it becomes a
      filename on disk under <code>$SHEP_HOME/logs</code> and is reported by{" "}
      <code>shep flock</code>, <code>shep describe</code> and{" "}
      <code>shep lookout</code>, which is four more places than the two this
      design puts a plaintext secret in.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="Flockfile.toml">{flockfileSnippet}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      <code>{'{{secret:vercel/API_KEY}}'}</code> is the other shape: a
      namespace and a slash before the key, reading what a provider dog
      registered as <code>vercel</code> has pushed rather than the
      operator's own store. More on that below.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock>{spawnedEnv}</CodeBlock>
    <p class="fine">
      <code>SHEP_ENVIRONMENT</code> is injected on every sheep and every
      dog, next to <code>SHEP_NAME</code> and <code>SHEP_INSTANCE</code>,
      and cannot be set by hand in <code>env</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>Environments and the all slot</h2>
    <p>
      A key can hold a different value per environment, so{" "}
      <code>production</code> and <code>staging</code> differ without two
      Flockfiles. A sheep's own environment comes from its{" "}
      <code>environment</code> field; unset, it falls back to{" "}
      <code>[daemon] environment</code> in <code>shep.toml</code>, which
      defaults to <code>production</code>. Neither may be <code>all</code>:
      that name is reserved for the slot every environment falls back to,
      so a value set with no <code>--env</code> covers every environment at
      once instead of being typed out per environment.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      A sheep's <code>environment</code> decides what every reference in it
      resolved to, and that is baked into the child at exec, so changing it
      on a running sheep parks until the next spawn the same way{" "}
      <code>cwd</code> does. See <a href="/docs/overrides">Overrides</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      Keys and environment names share one grammar: 1 to 128 bytes of
      letters, digits, <code>.</code>, <code>_</code> or <code>-</code>,
      never starting with a dot. Neither can hold the <code>/</code> a
      namespaced reference splits on.
    </p>
    <div class="rows">
      <div class="row"><span>exact environment</span><span>Used first, if the store has a value for it.</span></div>
      <div class="row"><span><code>all</code></span><span>Used when the sheep's own environment has nothing.</span></div>
      <div class="row"><span>anything else</span><span>Never. There is no fallback to a different named environment.</span></div>
    </div>
    <Callout variant="careful">
      That third row is the point of the whole design. A rule that filled
      an empty <code>staging</code> slot from <code>production</code>'s
      value would hand a live credential to staging the first day somebody
      forgot to set one. <code>shep secret get</code> and{" "}
      <code>{'{{secret:...}}'}</code> both stop at exact-environment, then{" "}
      <code>all</code>, then refuse.
    </Callout>

    <h2>shep secret</h2>
    <p>
      Four subcommands, working with no shepherd running, the same as{" "}
      <code>shep set</code>/<code>get</code>/<code>unset</code>:
    </p>
    <div class="rows">
      <div class="row"><span><code>set</code></span><span>Store a value under a key and an environment.</span></div>
      <div class="row"><span><code>get</code></span><span>Print a value back, gated below.</span></div>
      <div class="row"><span><code>unset</code></span><span>Remove one slot.</span></div>
      <div class="row"><span><code>list</code></span><span>Names keys and their environments. Never a value.</span></div>
    </div>
    <p>
      <code>set</code> takes the value as a positional argument by default,
      which puts it in <code>ps</code> output and in this shell's history
      for as long as the command runs. Prefer <code>--stdin</code> for
      anything that is actually a credential:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock>{cliDemo}</CodeBlock>
    <p class="fine">
      <code>--env</code> is exact: <code>unset DB_PASSWORD --env staging</code>{" "}
      touches the <code>staging</code> slot alone and leaves <code>all</code>{" "}
      standing. Omit <code>--env</code> on <code>set</code> or{" "}
      <code>unset</code> and the <code>all</code> slot is what changes.
    </p>

    <h2>Reading a value back</h2>
    <p>
      <code>get</code> needs <code>[secrets] allow_read = true</code> in{" "}
      <code>shep.toml</code>, default false, the same shape{" "}
      <a href="/docs/whistle">whistle</a>'s <code>allow_control</code> gates
      a different kind of access with:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="shep.toml">{`[secrets]
allow_read = true`}</CodeBlock>
    <CodeBlock>{gateOpenDemo}</CodeBlock>
    <p class="fine">
      The honest counter is on the record: the file is <code>0600</code>{" "}
      and its owner can already read it with <code>cat</code>, so refusing
      to print is partly theatre. What the gate actually buys is that{" "}
      <code>cat</code> is a deliberate act and <code>shep secret get</code>{" "}
      is a command whose output can land in scrollback during a screen
      share. Turning it on is a decision rather than a default.
    </p>
    <p>
      Without <code>--env</code>, <code>get</code> resolves against{" "}
      <code>[daemon] environment</code>'s own default rather than any one
      sheep's: this verb takes no sheep name, so it has no Flockfile to
      read an override from. <code>--env</code> checks exactly one slot:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock>{unsetDemo}</CodeBlock>

    <h2>A refused spawn</h2>
    <p>
      A <code>{'{{secret:...}}'}</code> that will not resolve refuses the
      spawn rather than reaching the child as literal text, and it refuses
      in one of two shapes depending on whether a namespace is named.
    </p>
    <div class="rows">
      <div class="row"><span>a key nobody has set</span><span>Nothing but a person will ever fill this in. <code>Errored</code> at once, no retry. The message names the key and the environment it looked in.</span></div>
      <div class="row"><span>a namespace no dog has pushed this environment for</span><span>The provider dog may just not be up yet, or may have pushed another environment first. <code>waiting-restart</code>, retried on the ordinary restart backoff.</span></div>
    </div>
    <p>
      <code>cron</code> below sets <code>environment = "staging"</code>{" "}
      and reads a key nobody has set. <code>worker</code> reads the{" "}
      <code>vercel</code> namespace from above, and no dog has pushed to it
      yet:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="Flockfile.toml">{cronSnippet}</CodeBlock>
    <p>Starting both refuses one spawn and defers the other:</p>
    <CodeBlock>{refusalLog}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      <code>shep describe</code> answers the question a stuck sheep raises
      without reading a Flockfile and the store side by side: which
      references this sheep's config carries, resolved in its own
      environment, and whether each one currently resolves.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock>{describeDemo}</CodeBlock>
    <p class="fine">
      <code>not cached</code> is not proof the running shepherd lacks the
      value too, only that the local cache file this verb reads does not
      have it: a dog pushing with <code>persist = false</code> never
      reaches that file. See below.
    </p>

    <h2>What a provider dog does</h2>
    <p>
      A dog cannot be asked for a value at spawn time, because assembling a
      spawn is the hottest path shep has and a socket round trip on every
      instance of every app would put a network call in the middle of it.
      So a provider dog pushes instead: <code>Request::PutSecrets</code>{" "}
      sends every value it holds for one namespace and one environment,
      replacing whatever that pair held before rather than merging into it,
      so a key the provider deleted disappears here on the next push too.
    </p>
    <p>
      One push covers one pair, which is what the second refusal shape above
      is keyed on. A dog that polls <code>production</code> and then{" "}
      <code>staging</code> has said nothing about staging until the second
      push lands, so a staging sheep starting in between waits on the restart
      ladder rather than erroring for good. An empty push counts: a dog
      saying it holds nothing for a pair is an answer, and a key missing
      after it is a person's to fix.
    </p>
    <Callout variant="careful">
      A namespace is bookkeeping, not authorization. The name a dog claims
      is self-declared, and shep has no portable way to check it against
      the process on the other end of the connection. The real boundary is
      the control socket itself, under <code>$SHEP_HOME</code> at{" "}
      <code>0700</code>: anything that can already open it can start an
      arbitrary process as the shepherd's own user, which is a wider door
      than any namespace could close. Namespaces keep one provider's values
      from colliding with another's. They are not a permission system.
    </Callout>
    <p>
      Pushed values are cached to <code>$SHEP_HOME/secrets-cache.json</code>{" "}
      by default, so a reboot does not leave every provider-backed sheep
      waiting on a dog and a network round trip before it can start.{" "}
      <code>persist = false</code> in the dog's own section of{" "}
      <code>dogs.toml</code> turns that off per provider, for an operator
      who has decided the risk of a cached copy outweighs the wait:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="dogs.toml">{`[vercel]
persist = false`}</CodeBlock>

    <h2>Why there is no encryption</h2>
    <p>
      <code>secrets.json</code> is plain JSON at <code>0600</code>, and
      that is not an oversight. Encrypting it would need a key stored
      somewhere shep can read at boot, on the same host, by the same
      process, at the same mode: a second file to lose rather than a
      second factor. The attacker this would defend against, one who can
      read <code>$SHEP_HOME</code> but not a key sitting beside it inside
      the same <code>0700</code> directory, does not exist. A credential
      that genuinely needs key custody belongs behind a provider dog,
      which can hold it wherever it likes before pushing a value across.
    </p>

